how are you doing today sir  i'm good good to see you nice to see you  um i really mean this sincerely i loved  this movie um and i want to thank you  for making it i felt like i was watching  jonathan on screen and it really meant a  lot to me  thank you i saw you a very kind tweet i  really appreciate that  yeah man i i it meant a lot to me um i  have a million questions and not enough  time so i'm gonna hopefully it's gonna  you know i can get through this um you  got to go to the library of congress and  see all of jonathan's  stuff  and i'm just curious i know what  jonathan means to you what was that  actually like and did you like lose your  mind looking through it all  yeah well um on a purely selfish level  like the movie aside it was fascinating  to me as a songwriter to  see his process unfold in his notes and  he's a really different songwriter than  me one of the things he did that i've  never seen another composer do and i've  looked through other people's archives  i've read hammerstein's archives like i  like it at the library of congress um is  he would ask himself questions and that  was often the inspiration for some of  his most enduring songs the most famous  example is how do you measure a year and  there is a piece of paper at library  congress where it's like 52 weeks 365  days then he gets to 525  600 minutes and there's just a bunch of  circles around it  um but  often  he would he would write these questions  and then write the song to answer the  question he was asking himself  and i think it's so fitting that the  last song in tiktik boom is every lyric  is a different question because that's  exactly where he was in his life he was  questioning whether he wanted to do this  for a living he was questioning how he  would get back up from the ashes of  superbia and write the next one um and  and  is a self-portrait of an artist at a  crossroads um and and it's all there in  his notes and it's all there in his  music  if you had the opportunity to show  jonathan a scene from this movie which  one would you want to show him first  oh my god um that's a really great and  tough question i think it's a tie  probably between um  it's a three-way time between the  opening number um where he would get  glimpses of the moon dance and his  apartment and um his loved ones um and  the sunday sequence uh  he only ever heard sunday with him at  the piano and maybe roger bart singing  back up in the background and um i  really tried to build the chorus of his  dreams for that sequence that was my  thesis going into that number um and  then probably therapy um he'd be annoyed  that i cut into it um  and didn't let it just play unfinished  but i i think the turn at the end of  that scene um i think would surprise him  i i think you would enjoy it  i love talking about the editing process  because that's where it all comes  together um as this being your first  feature  what really surprised you about the  editing process that perhaps you weren't  expecting  um first of all i had two amazing  editors and it happened very organically  um  first andy weiss bloom  who is wes anderson's guy and darren  aronofsky guy and um  we were so coveted delayed um by the  time we got through our first screening  cut of this movie darren aronofsky came  calling and said i i need andy weisblud  back  and so uh andy went off to that project  and i was lucky enough that myron  kerstein who had edited in the heights  uh were free and he came  he came to to continue to investigate  the piece with me and and when i say it  came to like this is covid times they  lived in my house we edited this like in  my in my home and so um it was it was an  amazing period um where my kids just got  to know these editors as uncle andy and  myron and because he'd lived and ate  dinner with us  but also to your question  it was the process the part i knew the  least about going in as a first-time  filmmaker and the part that was most  analogous to what i actually know how to  do which is build a score and build a  theater score for an evening you're  playing with the same things you're  playing with tension and release and  surprise and when do i sit in a moment  and when do i explode a moment and when  do i keep it moving  when is the audience ahead of me um you  know the surprise is sort of the thing  sondheim is always telling every one of  his mentees of like keep surprising us  um that's the most important thing you  can do when you're building a score  and so  it was i found that it was a very  analogous skill set um in terms of how  to pace an evening  did you have an actual like much longer  cut of the movie or was it always around  this running time  um i think my rough cut which is just  everything assembled in order was  probably 220. um it was very important  for me that this is under two hours  the off-broadway version is  90 minutes um and this is uh this is in  we're in power 55 but that being said i  put stuff in that wasn't in the  off-broadway version there is  the swimming sequence which was cut for  on broadway there's play game which you  know is uh it's you know jonathan  writing hippo numbers in the 80s um and  so um i i really wanted to to really  just feel like an evening with jonathan  larson so it was it was really about  keeping that stuff uh really tight i  mean one of the most painful things that  was we did a fully dance choreographed  green green dress  sequence um and you can hear uh andrew  and alex singing it on the closing  credits because i wanted you to at least  have it on your soundtracks um  but you know that's one of the ones  where when jonathan comes back from  wherever he is to watch the movie he's  gonna be like why'd you cut that song  and i'm gonna go well because no story  happens during it so i i had josh henry  singing it out of the radio uh as as  they get it on  and  [Music]  it was  that was a tough cut but it also like  really um supercharged the pace of the  movie  i already have to stop i'm going to say  again thank you so much for making the  film and um seriously i loved it thank  you so much  you

